  "query_preview": "/Through Sunday/...\nIssued at 249 PM MDT Sun Jul 26 2026\n\nThe scorching heat continues across Colorado today with triple digit \nafternoon highs expected for the low elevations. Given midday \ntemperatures in the upper 90s to even a few 100s, it is likely that \nmultiple station daily records will either be tied or broken. Below \nis a sampling of forecast and record temperatures;\n\nCity\t\tForecast\t\tOld Record\n\nDenver: \t   102\t\t\t100 in 1910\nFort Collins:  102\t\t\t100 in 1910\nBoulder:\t   101\t\t\t 98 in 1935\nGreeley UNC:   105\t\t\t102 in 2003\nFort Morgan:   106\t\t\t106 in 1910\nAkron:\t\t   101\t\t\t104 in 2003\nJulesburg:\t   105\t\t\t105 in 1894\nLimon: \t\t   100\t \t\t 98 in 2023\n\nMonsoonal moisture has already facilitated the development of a few \nshowers and thunderstorms over the mountains. Just like yesterday, \nthese showers and storms will have the potential to produce gusty \noutflow winds up to 40 mph as they move off the foothills and \ndissipate over the Denver metro area in the late afternoon and \nevening. The development of showers and outflows could encourage \nsporadic periods of \"cooler\" breeziness that bring slight heat \nrelief. However, cloud cover could also work to trap heat near the \nsurface by preventing nighttime radiative cooling from occurring.\nAs such, we expect temperatures tonight to be rather warm, only \ndropping into the mid 70s in parts of the Denver metro.\n\nFor tomorrow, the center of the upper level anticyclone associated \nwith the longwave ridge over the western CONUS will be positioned \nover southeast Colorado. Very weak south to southwesterly flow aloft \nwill continue to allow monsoonal moisture to increase over the \nregion with precipitable water (PW) values that greatly exceed \nclimatological average (150%-200% of normal). For the Denver metro, \nthis means that PW values will range around 1.4\"-1.6\", with a few \nlong-range ensemble members suggesting as high as 1.8\". Over the \nmountains, PW values will be around 0.75\" both west and east of the \nContinental Divide. These anomalous PWs, combined with very slow \nstorm motions (< 15KT cloud layer winds), and long-skinny CAPE \nsounding profiles, will lead to an increased risk of flash flooding \nparticularly with any storms that become anchored to the high \nterrain. The only ingredient that does not seem to be favorable for \nefficient rainfall production is a very shallow warm cloud layer, \nwhich is surprising for the warm airmass in place.\n\nTuesday's setup looks to be very similar to Monday's. PWs will still \nbe around 150% of normal, and the warm cloud layer will perhaps a \nbit more favorable for heavy rain, as deep as 5,000ft. By Wednesday, \nPWs are still anomalously high although faster storm motions will be \nin place due to flow aloft becoming more zonal. Model guidance has \nalso suggested a weak shortwave trough passage on Wednesday \nafternoon, which could provide additional lift for showers and \nthunderstorms to develop.\n\nIn summary, Monday, Tuesday, and Wednesday exhibit almost all of the \nnecessary ingredients to support localized heavy rainfall with only \na few fluctuations. It is difficult to tell which of these days will \nhave the highest rain chance given the erratic nature of monsoonal \nstorms as well as terrain interactions. High temperatures will run \ncloser to normal (low to mid 90s over the plains) due to the \nincrease in cloud cover and isolated to scattered shower coverage \neach afternoon. Monsoonal moisture greatly decreases on Friday, \nand long- range ensembles suggest a warming trend for the weekend,\nwith ensemble mean temperatures reaching the triple digits over \nthe plains once again for Sunday.",
